<!-- GATE FIXTURES FOR cs-impl, not language coverage.
     Everything here exists to make a PARSER-LEVEL gate able to FAIL. It is
     ordinary, compiling C# — the point is not that the language is unusual, it
     is that the grammar has a known defect on it.

     `AsyncAsIdentifier.cs` targets the three-line `async` patch in the vendored
     tree-sitter-c-sharp fork. Measured by cs-oracle: `async` used as an
     ordinary identifier in expression position is an ERROR in every published
     grammar version, it accounts for 25.1% of parse errors on 0.23.1 and 65.1%
     on 0.23.5, and it TRUNCATES — one bad occurrence mid-file took a 50-class
     file down to 25 recovered classes and a real file from corpus stratum
     linq-heavy-A down to 0 of 9
     methods. Patched, the fork goes 8.62% -> 3.01% errors and 6.44% -> 0.37%
     declarations lost.

     BUILDING-A-PARSER.md section 11: before trusting a null result, make the
     check produce a non-null one on purpose. A gate asserting "the async patch
     is present" cannot be shown to fail without a file it fails on. This is
     that file. Revert the patch and the gate must go red; if it stays green it
     was never testing anything. -->
<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- a successful build is the expected outcome: this is legal C# -->
    <CsFixtureCompiles>true</CsFixtureCompiles>
    <CsFixtureVerifiable>true</CsFixtureVerifiable>
    <CsFixtureRole>parser-gate</CsFixtureRole>
    <TargetFramework>net8.0</TargetFramework>
    <LangVersion>12</LangVersion>
    <Nullable>disable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>disable</ImplicitUsings>
    <DefineConstants></DefineConstants>
    <OutputType>Library</OutputType>
    <AssemblyName>Fixtures.GrammarGate</AssemblyName>
    <RootNamespace>Fixtures.GrammarGate</RootNamespace>
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CS0219;CS1998;CS0168</NoWarn>
  </PropertyGroup>
</Project>
